I. INTRODUCTION

As electricity use and barriers to secure and resilient transmission around the globe continue to grow, many areas are concerned about their abilities to continue to match electrical supply and demand. Renewable energy generation technologies can help to address energy security, economics, and resilience concerns, but many are variable, which can make it more difficult to guarantee enough supply at a given time. Furthermore, the electrical transmission system in the United States operates near its limit in many cases, making it difficult to transmit electricity from the source of the generation to the load even when renewably-generated electricity is available somewhere [1]. The recent National Transmission Needs Study [1] outlines key national needs related to the U.S. transmission infrastructure, demonstrating the challenge of increasing renewable energy generation using current strategies.

